      "text": "We start by writing the decimal representation of \\( \\frac{1}{7} \\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{7} = 0.\\overline{142857}\n\\]\n\nThis shows that the decimal repeats every 6 digits: \\(142857\\). To find the 100th digit after the decimal point, we need to determine the position of the 100th digit within the repeating cycle.\n\nFirst, we calculate the remainder when 100 is divided by 6:\n\n\\[\n100 \\div 6 = 16 \\text{ remainder } 4\n\\]\n\nThis tells us that 100 is equivalent to 4 in the repeating cycle \\(142857\\). So, the 100th digit corresponds to the 4th digit in the repeating sequence \\(142857\\).\n\nBy examining the sequence \\(142857\\), we see that the 4th digit is \\(8\\).\n\nThus, the 100th digit after the decimal point of the decimal representation of \\( \\frac{1}{7} \\) is \\(\\boxed{8}\\).",
