      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the 100th digit after the decimal point of the decimal representation of \\( \\frac{1}{7} \\), we start by calculating the decimal form of \\( \\frac{1}{7} \\).\n\nFirst, let's perform the division of 1 by 7:\n\n\\[\n1 \\div 7 = 0.142857142857\\ldots\n\\]\n\nWe observe that the decimal repeats every 6 digits: \\( 0.142857 \\).\n\nTo determine the 100th digit, we need to find the position of the 100th digit within the repeating sequence. We do this by finding the remainder when 100 is divided by 6:\n\n\\[\n100 \\div 6 = 16 \\text{ remainder } 4\n\\]\n\nThe quotient is 16, which tells us that the digits repeat every 6 digits, and the remainder is 4. This means that the 100th digit corresponds to the 4th digit in the repeating sequence \\( 0.142857 \\).\n\nLooking at the repeating sequence \\( 0.142857 \\), we see that the 4th digit is 2.\n\nTherefore, the 100th digit after the decimal point of the decimal representation of \\( \\frac{1}{7} \\) is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).",
