      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the 100th digit after the decimal point of the decimal representation of \\( \\frac{1}{7} \\), we need to determine the repeating sequence of the decimal expansion of \\( \\frac{1}{7} \\).\n\nFirst, we perform the long division of 1 by 7:\n\n1. \\( 1 \\div 7 = 0. \\) (Since 1 is less than 7, the quotient is 0, and we write 0 as the integer part.)\n2. Now we consider \\( 10 \\div 7 \\):\n   \\[\n   10 \\div 7 = 1 \\text{ remainder } 3 \\implies 10 = 7 \\times 1 + 3\n   \\]\n   So, the first digit after the decimal point is 1, and we have a remainder of 3.\n3. Now we consider \\( 30 \\div 7 \\):\n   \\[\n   30 \\div 7 = 4 \\text{ remainder } 2 \\implies 30 = 7 \\times 4 + 2\n   \\]\n   So, the next digit after the decimal point is 4, and we have a remainder of 2.\n4. Now we consider \\( 20 \\div 7 \\):\n   \\[\n   20 \\div 7 = 2 \\text{ remainder } 6 \\implies 20 = 7 \\times 2 + 6\n   \\]\n   So, the next digit after the decimal point is 2, and we have a remainder of 6.\n5. Now we consider \\( 60 \\div 7 \\):\n   \\[\n   60 \\div 7 = 8 \\text{ remainder } 4 \\implies 60 = 7 \\times 8 + 4\n   \\]\n   So, the next digit after the decimal point is 8, and we have a remainder of 4.\n6. Now we consider \\( 40 \\div 7 \\):\n   \\[\n   40 \\div 7 = 5 \\text{ remainder } 5 \\implies 40 = 7 \\times 5 + 5\n   \\]\n   So, the next digit after the decimal point is 5, and we have a remainder of 5.\n7. Now we consider \\( 50 \\div 7 \\):\n   \\[\n   50 \\div 7 = 7 \\text{ remainder } 1 \\implies 50 = 7 \\times 7 + 1\n   \\]\n   So, the next digit after the decimal point is 1, and we have a remainder of 1, which is the same as the original remainder we started with. This means the decimal expansion repeats from this point.\n\nThus, the repeating sequence of the decimal expansion of \\( \\frac{1}{7} \\) is \\( 142857 \\), which has a length of 6.\n\nTo find the 100th digit after the decimal point, we need to determine the position of the 100th digit within the repeating sequence. We do this by finding the remainder when 100 is divided by 6:\n\\[\n100 \\div 6 = 16 \\text{ remainder } 4\n\\]\nThis means that the 100th digit corresponds to the 4th digit in the repeating sequence \\( 142857 \\). The 4th digit in the sequence \\( 142857 \\) is 2.\n\nTherefore, the 100th digit after the decimal point of the decimal representation of \\( \\frac{1}{7} \\) is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).",
